Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: "Did You Crack the Annealing Glass?"
- Story & Context: At 2:14 AM outside Moradabad, an emergency brake shudder sent an immersion water-heater flying into Jasleen’s battery-powered sampler, frying the input stage and causing her looped dholak sample to emit a warm, crackling distortion that the entire sleeper carriage immediately fell in love with. Commuters pinned their shawls over the rattling berth door to seal in the heat, passing around plastic cups of ginger tea while Jasleen hummed the phrase "Hold the draft before the kettle spills again" over the thick, bowed drone of a double bass leaning against the corridor bulkhead. Within an hour, three strangers had plugged their pocket synths into a communal headphone brick, turning an icy transit breakdown into the foundational jam of the winter circuit.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro film shot through a railway window pane displaying hairline spiderweb cracks, catching the warm amber and cobalt light of a train interior, with silhouettes of pocket synthesizers and steam rising from a clay chai cup, painterly and nostalgic lo-fi aesthetic.

Track 2: Harpreet’s Stained Flagon
- Story & Context: Harpreet spent the six-hour crawl through Jalandhar hiding in the luggage space of Bunk 34, obsessively tapping out slow-swing rhythm codes on a cracked pocket drum machine while nursing a bruised knuckle from an argument with his father over selling the family brass workshop. To keep his upright bass silent enough for the ticket collector, he wove strips of damp linen between the gut strings, producing an impossibly deep, percussive plunk that locked into the train's rhythmic iron rail-clatter. He whispered the hook "I kept the brass peg so you could hear my hands shake" into his phone's voice-memo mic, creating an aching, tape-hiss confession that became an anthem for runaway artisans across the Punjab corridor.
